

GETTY(8)            UNIX Programmer's Manual            GETTY(8)



NAME
     getty - set terminal modes, greet, exec login

SYNOPSIS
     getty [-T type] ttyname

DESCRIPTION
     Getty is invoked by init(8) once per terminal line listed in
     /etc/ttys, with the line already open on file descriptors 0,
     1 and 2.  Getty itself opens nothing.

     It sets the line to cooked mode with echo on (so the login
     name is echoed; only the password, read later by login, is
     not), prints /etc/issue if it exists, then prompts:

          login:

     A bare return re-prompts.  End of file on the line (the
     connection dropped) makes getty exit 1, so init respawns it
     for the next user.  Otherwise getty reads a name and execs

          /bin/login name

     The -T option names the terminal type of the line, and getty
     passes it straight through to login, which prefers it over
     the type in ttys(5).  telnetd(8) supplies it from the
     client's TERMINAL-TYPE negotiation; init(8) does not pass it,
     so a line in ttys(5) keeps the type recorded there.

     replacing itself -- getty's stack is freed before login's is
     allocated.

     FGX narrowings.  This is not full BSD getty(8): there is no
     gettytab, no baud rate negotiation, no modem control (carrier
     detect, DTR drop) and no parity autodetection.  The ttyname
     argument is accepted for compatibility with init's invocation
     but is otherwise unused -- the line is already open.

FILES
     /etc/issue            greeting printed before each prompt
     /etc/ttys             lines and programs init starts

SEE ALSO
     login(1)

BUGS
     Explicit mode-setting (rather than inheriting whatever the
     line was left in) matters because init respawns getty after
     any session that may have left the line raw -- an inherited
     raw line would swallow the login name with no echo and look
     like a dead terminal.



